      "    ul bird.", "options": ["were", "have been", "was", "are"], "correct_index": 0, "explanation": "\"While\" introduces a longer action in the Past Continuous tense. For \"we,\" the auxiliary verb is \"were.\""}, {"question": "We ___ find the hotel yesterday without a map.", "options": ["can", "could", "might", "were able to"], "correct_index": 3, "explanation": "\"Yesterday\" indicates a specific past achievement or success in doing something, for which \"were able to\" is used, not \"could.\""}, {"question": "I ___ working today because it's a holiday.", "options": ["don't", "am not", "wasn't", "didn't"], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "This describes a temporary situation for \"today,\" requiring the Present Continuous tense. The negative form for \"I\" is \"am not.\""}, {"question": "___ you listen to music every night?", "options": ["Did", "Have", "Are", "Do"], "correct_index": 3, "explanation": "\"Every night\" indicates a regular habit, requiring the Present Simple tense. For questions in Present Simple with \"you,\" the auxiliary verb is \"Do.\""}, {"question": "Water ___ at 100 degrees Celsius.", "options": ["boiled", "will boil", "boils", "is boiling"], "correct_index": 2, "explanation": "This is a scientific fact, requiring the Present Simple tense. For \"water\" (it), the verb takes an \"-s.\""}, {"question": "They were playing football when it ___ to rain.", "options": ["was starting", "started", "starts", "had started"], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "\"When\" introduces a shorter, interrupting action in the Past Simple tense that occurs during a longer action in the Past Continuous."}, {"question": "She ___ open the door yesterday because she lost the key.", "options": ["can't", "won't be able to", "couldn't", "might not"], "correct_index": 2, "explanation": "\"Yesterday\" indicates a past inability, so \"couldn't\" is the correct modal verb."}, {"question": "Why ___ you crying now?", "options": ["are", "do", "were", "did"], "correct_index": 0, "explanation": "\"Now\" indicates an action happening at the moment of speaking, requiring the Present Continuous tense. The auxiliary verb for \"you\" in Present Continuous is \"are.\""}, {"question": "He ___ speak three languages now.", "options": ["could", "can", "might", "will be able to"], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "\"Now\" indicates a present ability, so \"can\" is the correct modal verb."}, {"question": "I'm sorry, I ___ hear you right now.", "options": ["couldn't", "can't", "won't be able to", "might not"], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "\"Right now\" indicates a present inability, so \"can't\" is the correct modal verb."}, {"question": "What ___ you doing right now?", "options": ["do", "are", "did", "were"], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "\"Right now\" indicates an action happening at the moment of speaking, requiring the Present Continuous tense. The auxiliary verb for \"you\" in Present Continuous is \"are.\""}, {"question": "Where ___ they live?", "options": ["do", "did", "have", "are"], "correct_index": 0, "explanation": "This asks about a general fact (where they reside), requiring the Present Simple tense. For questions with \"they,\" the auxiliary verb is \"do.\""}, {"question": "Look! The birds ___.", "options": ["sang", "sing", "are singing", "have sung"], "correct_index": 2, "explanation": "\"Look!\" is an imperative that signals an action happening at the moment of speaking, requiring the Present Continuous tense."}, {"question": "The supermarket is ___ close as the bakery.", "options": ["as", "too", "less", "more"], "correct_index": 0, "explanation": "\"As...as\" is used to show that two things are equal in some way."}]}j‹/f                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    lo simultaneous longer actions in the Past Continuous tense."}, {"question": "I ___ my homework at the moment.", "options": ["am doing", "done", "do", "doing"], "correct_index": 0, "explanation": "\"At the moment\" indicates an action happening now, requiring the Present Continuous tense (subject + am/is/are + verb-ing)."}, {"question": "After a lot of practice, she ___ pass her driving test last week.", "options": ["could", "might", "can", "was able to"], "correct_index": 3, "explanation": "\"Last week\" indicates a specific past achievement or success in doing something, for which \"was able to\" (or \"managed to\") is used, not \"could.\""}, {"question": "I am ___ tall as my father now.", "options": ["too", "less", "as", "more"], "correct_index": 2, "explanation": "\"As...as\" is used to show that two things are equal in some way."}, {"question": "I ___ a shower when you knocked on the door.", "options": ["am taking", "was taking", "took", "had taken"], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "\"When you knocked\" (Past Simple) interrupted the longer action of \"I was taking a shower\" (Past Continuous)."}, {"question": "We were having dinner ___ the electricity stopped.", "options": ["during", "when", "as", "while"], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "\"When\" is used to introduce a shorter, interrupting action (Past Simple) that happens during a longer action (Past Continuous)."}, {"question": "When he was young, he ___ run very fast.", "options": ["may", "will be able to", "can", "could"], "correct_index": 3, "explanation": "\"When he was young\" indicates a past ability, so \"could\" is the correct modal verb."}, {"question": "___ you were sleeping, your brother washed the car.", "options": ["While", "During", "As soon as", "When"], "correct_index": 0, "explanation": "\"While\" is used to connect two simultaneous actions, one of which is often longer. Here, \"you were sleeping\" is a longer action."}, {"question": "My bedroom is ___ big as your bedroom. They are exactly the same size.", "options": ["as", "too", "less", "more"], "correct_index": 0, "explanation": "\"As...as\" is used to show that two things are equal in some way. \"They are exactly the same size\" confirms equality."}, {"question": "My mother ___ dinner at the moment.", "options": ["has cooked", "cooked", "is cooking", "cooks"], "correct_index": 2, "explanation": "\"At the moment\" indicates an action happening now, requiring the Present Continuous tense. For \"my mother\" (she), the auxiliary verb is \"is.\""}, {"question": "Silver is not ___ expensive as gold.", "options": ["less", "too", "more", "as"], "correct_index": 3, "explanation": "\"Not as...as\" is used to show that two things are not equal in some way."}, {"question": "If you heat ice, it ___ into water.", "options": ["melted", "melts", "will melt", "would melt"], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "The Zero Conditional describes general truths or scientific facts. Both clauses use the Present Simple tense."}, {"question": "If it rains tomorrow, we ___ go to the park.", "options": ["don't", "wouldn't", "didn't", "won't"], "correct_index": 3, "explanation": "The First Conditional describes a real possibility in the future. The \"if\" clause uses the Present Simple, and the main clause uses \"will\" + base verb (or \"won't\" for negative)."}, {"question": "They ___ walk after the accident last month.", "options": ["won't be able to", "might not", "couldn't", "can't"], "correct_index": 2, "explanation": "\"Last month\" indicates a past inability, so \"couldn't\" is the correct modal verb."}, {"question": "Learning English is not ___ hard as learning Chinese.", "options": ["too", "as", "more", "less"], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "\"Not as...as\" is used to show that two things are not equal in some way."}, {"question": "Winter in Spain is not ___ cold as in Canada.", "options": ["less", "more", "too", "as"], "correct_index": 3, "explanation": "\"Not as...as\" is used to show that two things are not equal in some way."}, {"question": "While we ___ walking in the park, we saw a beautif
