unki element qo'shish va eng yuqori ustuvorlikdagi elementni olish O(log n) vaqt oladi."}]}jЉџf   mdn't"], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "This states a general fact about penguins' present inability, so \"can't\" is the correct modal verb."}, {"question": "We ___ to school by bus every day.", "options": ["went", "goes", "are going", "go"], "correct_index": 3, "explanation": "\"Every day\" indicates a regular routine, requiring the Present Simple tense. For \"we,\" the base form of the verb is used."}, {"question": "Your cat is ___ beautiful as mine.", "options": ["less", "more", "too", "as"], "correct_index": 3, "explanation": "\"As...as\" is used to show that two things are equal in some way."}, {"question": "He ___ a book right now.", "options": ["is reading", "reads", "has read", "read"], "correct_index": 0, "explanation": "\"Right now\" indicates an action happening at the moment of speaking, requiring the Present Continuous tense (subject + am/is/are + verb-ing)."}, {"question": "My phone rang when I ___ driving.", "options": ["had been", "were", "am", "was"], "correct_index": 3, "explanation": "\"When my phone rang\" (Past Simple) interrupted the longer action of \"I was driving\" (Past Continuous)."}, {"question": "I have never ___ understand his accent.", "options": ["might", "could", "been able to", "can"], "correct_index": 2, "explanation": "In perfect tenses (like Present Perfect \"have never\"), \"be able to\" is used instead of \"can/could\" to express ability. The past participle of \"be\" is \"been.\""}, {"question": "He doesn't run ___ fast as his brother.", "options": ["more", "less", "too", "as"], "correct_index": 3, "explanation": "\"Not as...as\" is used to show that two things are not equal in some way."}, {"question": "I was watching TV ___ the telephone rang.", "options": ["as", "when", "during", "while"], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "\"When\" is used to introduce a shorter, interrupting action (Past Simple) that happens during a longer action (Past Continuous)."}, {"question": "___ you speak English when you started school?", "options": ["Could", "Were", "Can", "Did"], "correct_index": 0, "explanation": "\"When you started school\" indicates a past ability, so \"Could\" is the correct modal verb for the question."}, {"question": "They ___ coffee every morning.", "options": ["drank", "are drinking", "drinks", "drink"], "correct_index": 3, "explanation": "\"Every morning\" indicates a regular routine, requiring the Present Simple tense. For \"they,\" the base form of the verb is used."}, {"question": "He ___ like milk.", "options": ["isn't", "doesn't", "wasn't", "don't"], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "This expresses a general preference, requiring the Present Simple tense. For negative statements with \"he,\" the auxiliary verb is \"doesn't.\""}, {"question": "Tomorrow, I ___ finish this project.", "options": ["could", "will be able to", "can", "might"], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "\"Tomorrow\" indicates a future ability, so \"will be able to\" is the correct expression. \"Can\" is generally for present ability, and \"could\" for past or possibility."}, {"question": "I ___ swim when I was five years old.", "options": ["can", "could", "will be able to", "may"], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "\"When I was five years old\" indicates a past ability, so \"could\" is the correct modal verb."}, {"question": "My sister is not ___ old as me. She is younger.", "options": ["as", "more", "too", "less"], "correct_index": 0, "explanation": "\"Not as...as\" is used to show that two things are not equal in some way. \"She is younger\" confirms the inequality."}, {"question": "What ___ you doing when I called you yesterday?", "options": ["were", "are", "did", "was"], "correct_index": 0, "explanation": "This asks about an action in progress in the past, interrupted by another past action. The Past Continuous tense is formed with \"was/were\" + verb-ing. For \"you,\" \"were\" is used."}, {"que