as"], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "\"While\" introduces a longer action in the Past Continuous tense. For \"they,\" the auxiliary verb is \"were.\""}, {"question": "This film is ___ interesting as the book. Both are great.", "options": ["too", "as", "less", "more"], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "\"As...as\" is used to show that two things are equal in some way. \"Both are great\" confirms equality."}, {"question": "One day, humans ___ live on Mars.", "options": ["could", "can", "will be able to", "might"], "correct_index": 2, "explanation": "\"One day\" indicates a future ability or possibility, so \"will be able to\" is the most appropriate expression."}, {"question": "This test was not ___ difficult as the last one. It was easier.", "options": ["too", "less", "more", "as"], "correct_index": 3, "explanation": "\"Not as...as\" is used to show that two things are not equal in some way. \"It was easier\" confirms the inequality."}, {"question": "Listen! Someone ___ the piano.", "options": ["plays", "has played", "played", "is playing"], "correct_index": 3, "explanation": "\"Listen!\" is an imperative that signals an action happening at the moment of speaking, requiring the Present Continuous tense. \"Someone\" takes a singular verb."}, {"question": "Today is not ___ sunny as yesterday.", "options": ["less", "as", "more", "too"], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "\"Not as...as\" is used to show that two things are not equal in some way."}, {"question": "___ you help me with this heavy bag, please?", "options": ["Can", "May", "Could", "Will"], "correct_index": 2, "explanation": "\"Could\" is often used for polite requests. \"Can\" is also possible but less formal."}, {"question": "This tea is ___ hot as coffee.", "options": ["too", "more", "as", "less"], "correct_index": 2, "explanation": "\"As...as\" is used to show that two things are equal in some way."}, {"question": "This phone is ___ expensive as that one. They cost the same.", "options": ["more", "